7.2 Layer-by-layer diagnostic scope

Where supported by the actual environment and permitted by the existing execution policy, examine:

Layer 1 — Local interfaces

Actual interface names, status, configured addresses, and relevant scope.

Whether the observed runtime corresponds to the local workstation or a server/container.

Layer 2 — Local link and neighbor information

Relevant ARP or IPv6 Neighbor Discovery entries when accessible.

Gateway MAC/address association where genuinely observable.

Report unavailable neighbor information as unavailable, not failed.

Layer 3 — Routing and gateway

The active IPv4/IPv6 default route.

The actual next hop and interface, if verified.

The gateway's reachability where a valid gateway address is available.

Whether route collection or reachability checking is unavailable.

Layer 3/4 — Upstream reachability

Whether an outbound route and actual external connections can be established.

Use approved, configured connectivity targets or the existing safe diagnostic target policy.

Do not automatically inherit Google or a prior mail-server target merely because it was previously tested.

DNS — Name resolution

Verify a relevant system resolver or known test name using the actual DNS capability.

Keep resolver configuration, successful DNS lookup, and general DNS health distinct.

TCP/TLS/HTTP — External service tests

Use existing supported probes against suitable approved targets to establish external service reachability.

Report exactly which endpoints and ports were tested.

Do not claim the entire ISP link is healthy just because a single HTTPS endpoint responds.

Path trace — When supported

Use an existing traceroute or equivalent capability if it is useful and permitted.

Preserve intermediate hop observations and timeouts.

Do not conclude that the ISP connection failed solely because a router does not answer traceroute probes.

Do not run every possible probe by default. Choose the smallest set of appropriate checks that can answer the request, and explain any relevant omitted layer.

7.3 ISP attribution must be evidence-based

The assistant can investigate local routing and external connectivity. It must not claim to have identified an ISP outage merely because one target times out.

A blocked gateway ping may coexist with working upstream traffic. An unresponsive intermediate traceroute hop may simply suppress ICMP replies. A restricted container may not expose the host's actual network path.

Use conclusions such as:

Local routing information is available/unavailable.

The gateway responded/did not respond to the selected probe.

An external target was reachable/unreachable by the tested method.

DNS resolved/failed for the specific tested name.

The ISP boundary could not be conclusively identified from the accessible evidence.

More evidence is required to determine whether the ISP is responsible.

If no route is visible from the runtime, continue only those checks that can meaningfully run from that environment, label the unavailable layer, and avoid claiming that the user's own ISP has been tested.

Do not fabricate a gateway, ISP IP, public IP, or traceroute hop.

7.4 Acceptance for the layered path request

